p2u: 1
deck:
  title: Python Basics
  subtitle: Explain like I'm 5 — your first steps in code
  theme: writebook
  meta:
    audience: Complete beginners
slides:
  - layout: title
    title: Python Basics
    subtitle: Explain like I'm 5 — your first steps in code
    notes: |
      Welcome! No experience needed. We'll learn what code is and a few simple
      Python ideas by comparing them to everyday things.

  - layout: content
    title: What Is Python?
    body: |
      Python is how we give a computer **step-by-step instructions** — like a
      recipe tells a cook what to do.

      - A computer is very fast but it does **exactly** what you say
      - Python is written in words that look almost like English
      - You write the steps once, and the computer can repeat them forever
    notes: |
      Emphasise that computers are not smart; they follow instructions literally.
      Python is popular because its instructions are easy to read.

  - layout: diagram
    title: A Recipe for the Computer
    blocks:
      - kind: diagram
        lang: d2-sketch
        source: |
          direction: right
          you: You
          python: Python
          computer: Computer
          you -> python: write the steps
          python -> computer: run the steps
          computer -> you: show the answer
    notes: You write the recipe in Python; Python passes it to the computer.

  - layout: content
    title: Variables Are Labelled Boxes
    body: |
      A **variable** is a box with a name on it. You put something inside, and
      later you can look inside by saying the name.

      - `name = "Ada"` puts the word **Ada** in a box called `name`
      - `age = 5` puts the number **5** in a box called `age`
      - The `=` sign means "put this in the box", not "is equal to"
    notes: |
      The box analogy is key. `=` is assignment. We'll contrast with `==` later
      when we talk about asking questions.

  - layout: code-split
    title: Saying Hello
    code:
      kind: code
      lang: python
      source: |
        name = "Ada"
        print("Hello,", name)

        age = 5
        print("Next year you will be", age + 1)
    prose: |
      `print(...)` tells Python to **show something on the screen**.

      Notice `age + 1`: Python can do the maths for you and show `6`.
    notes: |
      Run this live if possible. Point out that text needs quotes but numbers
      do not.

  - layout: content
    title: "Types: Numbers, Words, and Yes/No"
    body: |
      Everything in a box has a **type** — what kind of thing it is.

      - `int` — whole numbers like `5`, `42`
      - `float` — numbers with a dot like `3.14`
      - `str` — text, called a **string**, like `"hello"`
      - `bool` — only `True` or `False`, like a light switch
    notes: |
      Keep it light. The point is that Python cares about the kind of value so
      it knows what it is allowed to do with it.

  - layout: code-split
    title: Lists Are Rows of Boxes
    code:
      kind: code
      lang: python
      source: |
        fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]

        print(fruits[0])
        print(len(fruits))

        fruits.append("date")
        print(fruits)
    prose: |
      A **list** keeps many things together in order — like a shopping list.

      - `fruits[0]` means "the first item" (we start counting at `0`)
      - `len(fruits)` counts how many items there are
      - `fruits.append("date")` adds one more to the end
    notes: |
      Zero-based counting is surprising for beginners — call it out.

  - layout: code-split
    title: Loops Repeat Things
    code:
      kind: code
      lang: python
      source: |
        fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]

        for fruit in fruits:
            print("I like", fruit)
    prose: |
      A **loop** says "do this for each thing in the list".

      Instead of writing three prints, we write one and let Python repeat it.
      The indented line is the part that repeats.
    notes: |
      Indentation is how Python knows what is inside the loop.

  - layout: diagram
    title: How a Loop Thinks
    blocks:
      - kind: diagram
        lang: d2-sketch
        source: |
          direction: right
          start: Start
          more: "More items?"
          step: "Do the step"
          done: Done
          start -> more
          more -> step: yes
          step -> more
          more -> done: no
    notes: The loop keeps coming back to the question until the list is empty.

  - layout: content
    title: Making Choices with if
    body: |
      An **if** statement asks a question and picks what to do next.

      - `if raining: take umbrella()` — only when it is raining
      - `else: wear sunglasses()` — every other time

      Asking a question uses two equals signs: `if age == 5:` means
      "is the box called age equal to 5?"
    notes: |
      Contrast `=` (put in box) with `==` (ask a question). This is a classic
      beginner trip-up.

  - layout: code-split
    title: Functions Are Little Machines
    code:
      kind: code
      lang: python
      source: |
        def greet(person):
            return "Hello, " + person

        message = greet("Ada")
        print(message)
    prose: |
      A **function** is a machine: you give it something, it does a job, and it
      hands something back.

      - `def` means "I am building a machine"
      - `person` is what you feed in
      - `return` is what the machine hands back
    notes: |
      Functions let us write a step once and use it many times.

  - layout: diagram
    title: Inside a Function
    blocks:
      - kind: diagram
        lang: d2-sketch
        source: |
          direction: right
          input: "\"Ada\""
          machine: "greet"
          output: "\"Hello, Ada\""
          input -> machine -> output
    notes: Input goes in, the function runs, output comes out.

  - layout: quote
    title: Why Python?
    blocks:
      - kind: quote
        text: Code is read far more often than it is written.
        attribution: The Zen of Python
    notes: |
      Python is designed to be easy to read, which makes it a great first
      language.

  - layout: recap
    title: Recap
    body: |
      - Python gives a computer step-by-step instructions
      - Variables are labelled boxes that hold values
      - Types describe what kind of value is inside
      - Lists keep many things together, and loops repeat work
      - `if` makes choices, and functions are reusable machines